PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The structure in C remains the only one that aligns every requirement without contradiction: Alex and Tyler as sole directors plus equal voting shares (Class A/B) deliver 50/50 control; Alex’s Class A shares are deliberately non-dividend so he can be paid exclusively by salary; Tyler’s Class B shares permit dividends; and the explicit non-voting Class C class supplies the vehicle for future silent investors.  

The residual-asset limitation on Class C is a legitimate drafting concern for real-world investors, yet it does not disqualify the option under the stated criteria—none of the other choices simultaneously satisfy all four points. B reverses the dividend assignment, D supplies no non-voting class at all, A/E either equalize dividend rights or add extra directors, and the superfluous Class D or issuance to Tyler’s wife in C do not impair functionality.  

Thus C is still the unique match; the practical investor-rights issue can be noted for later rounds if needed.}

